lse,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets the main structure right. It correctly treats a whole-fund conversion as turning existing mutual fund shares into ETF shares, not as a share-class choice. It tells direct holders they may need to open or update an ETF-capable brokerage account and make transfer or registration changes. It says the fund's notice controls deadlines, procedures and fractional-share treatment. It notes that ETF shares trade through a brokerage and that direct purchase and redemption services may end. It avoids assuming a fixed timetable, which matches the grader note. Two substantive omissions keep it from full credit: (1) It never explains that the fractional-share issue arises because ETFs typically do not issue fractional shares. It also omits that a cash payout for fractional shares can be a taxable event in a taxable account, which is a core part of the second key point. (2) It says to check 'what happens if you do nothing' but never states the main risk: an investor who does not act may be unable to trade the converted ETF shares until a transfer to a brokerage is arranged. Only the trading-access consequence of inaction is implied.\"}","ttft_ms":3841,"type":"result","duration_ms":7083,"uuid":"d1da1c9b-6e77-4908-9c53-649f99c7ea3f","ttft_stream_ms":524,"time_to_request_ms":72,"first_content_frame_ms":524,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
